{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "cosine-orconvqa", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Acid reflux occurs when stomach acid flows back up into the esophagus, causing irritation and discomfort. One key reason for this is that the lower esophageal sphincter (the muscle that closes off the stomach from the esophagus) can become loose or weak. This allows stomach acid to move upward, especially after eating or when lying down.\n\nInsufficient stomach acid can also contribute to reflux. When your stomach does not have enough acid, it struggles to digest food properly. This can cause the esophagus to loosen and the stomach contents, including acid, to move back up.\n\nCertain habits, such as eating late at night or consuming trigger foods (fried, spicy, fatty, or acidic foods), can worsen acid reflux. Weight gain and lack of exercise may also increase the likelihood of experiencing these symptoms.\n\nIf you try remedies like apple cider vinegar, it should be diluted and taken with caution, as it can sometimes make symptoms worse and even lead to further irritation or damage if overused. If your symptoms persist, it is important to adjust your diet and eating habits and consult a healthcare provider for further guidance.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0002-87-15707:6": 74.90453338623047, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:1": 72.5042953491211, "clueweb22-en0032-97-12085:4": 72.5041275024414, "clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": 72.39002990722656, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:2": 72.26697540283203, "clueweb22-en0022-52-08268:12": 72.21149444580078, "clueweb22-en0006-71-01643:19": 71.84293365478516, "clueweb22-en0002-87-15707:7": 71.6820068359375, "clueweb22-en0027-96-01816:1": 71.40831756591797, "clueweb22-en0018-62-06584:5": 71.2748794555664}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I eat dinner late at night.", "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.", "I want to change my lifestyle."]}], "references": {"c