{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "cosine-orconvqa", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Thank you for clarifying your question. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) and H2 blockers both reduce stomach acid but work in different ways:\n\n- PPIs (such as omeprazole or lansoprazole) block the proton pumps in the stomach lining, which are responsible for producing acid. This leads to a more significant and longer-lasting reduction in stomach acid production.\n- H2 blockers (like cimetidine or famotidine) block histamine receptors in stomach cells. Histamine is a chemical that signals the stomach to produce acid, so blocking these receptors decreases acid production, but not as strongly or as long as PPIs.\n\nGiven your slightly impaired kidney function, it is important to note that studies have shown PPIs are associated with a higher risk of worsening kidney function compared to H2 blockers. Therefore, doctors often prefer H2 blockers or nonmedication strategies when possible, especially for those with kidney concerns.\n\nIf you need medication for acid reflux, discuss with your healthcare provider which option is best for your situation, and make sure your kidney function is monitored regularly.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0036-68-02735:11": 40.26036071777344, "clueweb22-en0027-15-02848:1": 40.153743743896484, "clueweb22-en0007-00-09782:3": 39.64455795288086, "clueweb22-en0025-37-14848:4": 39.56544494628906, "clueweb22-en0002-04-09789:11": 39.396060943603516, "clueweb22-en0027-15-02848:2": 38.49773025512695, "clueweb22-en0025-12-11981:9": 37.82447814941406, "clueweb22-en0002-04-09789:12": 37.732234954833984, "clueweb22-en0038-05-11085:7": 37.666542053222656, "clueweb22-en0038-05-11085:8": 37.66575241088867}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.", "I want to change my lifestyle."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0036-68-02735:11": 40.26036071777344, "cl