{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "cosine-orconvqa", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly. The tendons in your fingers are responsible for transmitting the force from muscles in your forearm and wrist to your fingers, allowing them to bend and grip\u2014an essential motion for climbing. Flexor tendon injuries are common among climbers and can cause pain and limited movement. These injuries often occur from intense use, such as during difficult climbs or when using crimp grips.\n\nSymptoms of a tendon-related injury may include pain along the finger, discomfort when bending, or a feeling of tension. Sometimes, soreness may persist for weeks and gradually subside with rest and gentle rehabilitation. However, if you experience persistent pain, swelling, or an inability to move your finger, it is advisable to seek medical evaluation to rule out more serious injuries.\n\nFor now, it is recommended to rest the affected finger, avoid activities that cause pain, and consider gentle compression or taping when you resume climbing. If the pain continues or worsens, consult a hand specialist for proper diagnosis and treatment.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 44.27030563354492, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:11": 43.9640998840332, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:7": 43.17910385131836, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:10": 43.13376235961914, "clueweb22-en0035-35-02982:2": 43.131248474121094, "clueweb22-en0005-98-01404:21": 41.826534271240234, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:13": 41.69287109375, "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:18": 41.69287109375, "clueweb22-en0001-34-18428:18": 41.639461517333984, "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:13": 41.6370964050293}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 44.27030563354492, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:11": 43.9640998840332, "clueweb22-en0005-83