{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "cosine-orconvqa", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly. Finger pain can manifest differently depending on the specific area affected:\n\n- **Tendon Pain**: Pain in the tendons, often felt on the palm side of your finger, is usually a deep, aching or sharp sensation, especially when bending or loading the finger. In climbers, this is common due to repetitive pulling movements. Swelling or tenderness along the tendon sheath may also occur.\n\n- **Pulley Injuries**: If the pain is localized along the finger (often near the base or middle), particularly when crimping or flexing, this could indicate a pulley injury. You might notice tenderness when pressing on the area and sometimes a popping sensation during the injury.\n\n- **Joint Pain**: Pain in the joints (the knuckles or where the finger bends) is usually sharp or aching and can be accompanied by swelling, stiffness, or reduced range of motion. This pain may persist after climbing, especially if there\u2019s inflammation.\n\n- **Ligament or Connective Tissue Pain**: This pain is often more diffuse, possibly with swelling, and can worsen with sideways movements or twisting.\n\nIf your pain is persistent, worsening, or associated with swelling, redness, or reduced function, a medical evaluation is recommended to rule out more serious tendon, ligament, or joint issues.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-75-20030:10": 32.16828155517578, "clueweb22-en0007-26-15376:2": 31.968271255493164, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:1": 31.80811882019043, "clueweb22-en0004-10-04480:0": 31.48576545715332, "clueweb22-en0015-51-03260:9": 30.777557373046875, "clueweb22-en0041-52-01391:0": 30.750032424926758, "clueweb22-en0002-28-11753:0": 30.66731834411621, "clueweb22-en0002-28-11753:2": 30.662221908569336, "clueweb22-en0007-75-20030:11": 30.61461639404297, "clueweb22-en0008-49-08589:1": 30.554780960083008}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing 