{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "cosine-orconvqa", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your symptoms\u2014pain at the front of the finger, especially during crimping, without visible bowstringing\u2014it is likely an A2 pulley strain rather than a complete rupture. The first and most important step is to rest the affected finger and avoid climbing activities, particularly those requiring a full crimp grip, as this increases the risk of aggravating the injury.\n\nIn the short term, apply ice to reduce swelling and consider anti-inflammatory measures if needed. Avoid taping healthy fingers, as this has been shown to be ineffective and potentially harmful, but for a confirmed pulley injury, H-taping is the most effective method for supporting the pulley and preventing tendon bowstringing during recovery.\n\nA thorough warm-up and proper climbing technique are essential when you return to activity. If pain, swelling, or stiffness persists, or if you have trouble bending or straightening your finger, consult a healthcare provider or hand therapist for further assessment and possible therapy. Gradual improvement typically occurs over 4 to 6 months, but persistent symptoms require professional evaluation.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 27.631420135498047, "clueweb22-en0032-72-00969:16": 26.456375122070312, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 24.785720825195312, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 24.659181594848633, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 24.07204818725586, "clueweb22-en0032-72-00969:15": 23.531816482543945, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 23.528505325317383, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 23.516414642333984, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 23.41838264465332, "clueweb22-en0042-73-03077:16": 23.397958755493164}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09