{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "cosine-orconvqa", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "If you prefer not to use supplements, taping remains a practical and widely used strategy among climbers for protecting injured pulleys and supporting recovery. To tape your finger for an A2 pulley strain, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Choose the Right Tape**: Use a thin, water-resistant athletic tape, about 1.25 cm (\u00bd inch) wide.\n2. **Positioning**: Slightly bend your finger (as if holding a climbing hold).\n3. **H-Method** (most common for A2 injuries):  \n   - Cut a strip of tape long enough to wrap twice around your finger.  \n   - Tear the tape in the middle of each end, creating an \u201cH.\u201d  \n   - Place the uncut middle section over the injured area (base of the finger), wrap the split ends diagonally above and below the joint, and then bring them to the palm side.  \n   - Secure firmly but not so tight as to restrict circulation.\n4. **General Tips**:  \n   - Change the tape regularly, especially after climbing or washing hands, to prevent infection and promote healing.\n   - Taping is mainly for protection and proprioception; it does not fully prevent re-injury, so continue to climb cautiously.\n\nIf pain persists or worsens, consider consulting a healthcare professional.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0037-12-04573:7": 25.650068283081055, "clueweb22-en0013-89-09846:1": 22.721933364868164, "clueweb22-en0013-89-09846:0": 22.393516540527344, "clueweb22-en0016-32-04397:9": 22.286375045776367, "clueweb22-en0019-33-03699:9": 22.057729721069336, "clueweb22-en0042-38-09520:4": 21.79822540283203, "clueweb22-en0032-76-04021:6": 21.755403518676758, "clueweb22-en0045-26-03968:16": 21.689388275146484, "clueweb22-en0022-81-09628:9": 21.625375747680664, "clueweb22-en0022-67-05290:4": 21.57997703552246}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb2