{"metadata": {"team_id": "guidance", "run_id": "cosine-orconvqa", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly. Managing interruptions during hyperfocus can be particularly challenging with ADHD, as it may take time to shift your attention away from deep concentration and then re-engage. Here are some strategies based on the information provided:\n\n1. **Scheduled Check-Ins:** Allocate specific times in your day to check emails or messages, rather than reacting to them as they come in. This helps you stay immersed in your work during hyperfocus periods.\n\n2. **Use Visual Reminders:** Place a visible note or set a subtle alarm to remind you of upcoming meetings or commitments. This helps prevent missing scheduled appointments without pulling you out of your flow unnecessarily.\n\n3. **Buffer Time:** Try to leave a short buffer window between intense work sessions and scheduled meetings. This allows your mind to transition out of hyperfocus gradually, reducing the risk of feeling disoriented or emotionally \u201ccrashed\u201d after being deeply engaged.\n\n4. **Single-Task Environment:** Since you enjoy quiet and work in a relatively distraction-free office, take advantage of this by silencing unnecessary notifications during periods of deep work. You can set your status to \u201cdo not disturb\u201d or close your email client temporarily.\n\n5. **Gentle Refocusing:** If an interruption does occur, jot down exactly where you left off before shifting your attention. This note will help you quickly re-engage with your original task after the distraction.\n\nWould you like tailored suggestions for handling specific types of interruptions, such as meetings or urgent emails?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0014-54-13628:2": 47.738487243652344, "clueweb22-en0004-49-00618:3": 46.673004150390625, "clueweb22-en0022-21-03294:11": 45.54828643798828, "clueweb22-en0006-32-02164:17": 44.121986389160156, "clueweb22-en0036-30-15322:2": 43.9